# Watching What the Agent Was Actually Doing

Switching to DQN was meant to fix a specific, narrow problem: repeated swaps.

What it actually did was change how this project understood reinforcement learning, not just how it implemented it.

---

## Code That Runs Is Not Code That Works

The first trained policy looked fine on paper.

It trained without errors, the loss curves moved, and the model saved and loaded correctly.

Watching it actually play was what showed the truth: it picked one guess and never deviated from it for the rest of the episode.

> A model that trains without error is not the same as a model that is doing the right thing.

The only way to tell the difference was to watch it play, not to read the training log.

This became the first real lesson of the RL work, and it repeated itself more than once:

generic fixes and generic advice "try a different algorithm, train longer, adjust a hyperparameter" do not diagnose a specific behavior.

Understanding why the agent kept repeating itself required reasoning about the actual mechanics of the environment:

the observation barely changed when a swap failed;

a deterministic policy has no way to behave differently on an input that looks the same as the one before it.

That explanation didn't come from a tip.

It came from tracing through what the network could and could not possibly tell apart.

---

## Every Real Fix Came From the Same Question

The pattern held for everything that followed.

Adding hot/cold signal to the observation worked because it answered a specific question about what the network was missing;

not because it was a generally recommended technique.

A single step of memory still allowed cycling between a small set of failed swaps;

That came from reasoning directly about wether the agent could distinguish between two different situations that would otherwise look identical to it;

not from a suggestion, but from asking the same question again: what does this input actually tell the network, and what does it hide from it.

> Understanding what the algorithm was thinking;

what its input let it see and what it couldn't did more for this project than any individual code change did on its own.

---

## Getting It to Play At All Was the Milestone

After all of that, the agent that came out the other side is not a strong player.

It uses its move history now, and it visibly tries different swaps instead of freezing on the first one;

which is a genuinely different agent from the one that started this stretch of the project;

but it is still far from competitive with the heuristic agents built earlier.

That is being treated as a real result, not a shortfall.

Getting something to attempt the puzzle at all in a way that shows room to improve rather than a hard failure;

was always going to be worth more at this stage than a highly tuned but opaque success would have been.

---

## What's Next: A Real Ceiling to Measure Against

The natural next comparison is a Knuth style optimal search;

the same idea flagged early in this project as a principled reference point;

back when the heuristic agents were first being benchmarked against each other.

Measuring the RL agent against an actual information theoretic optimum;

rather than only against the other heuristics, is the next step:

not to prove the RL approach superior, but to see how far apart learned and optimal actually are on this particular problem,

and what that gap suggests about the environment and reward design.

---

None of this was approached expecting the reinforcement learning attempt to succeed outright.

The point of this playground was never to arrive at the best possible agent on the first try;

it was to take an idea that existed only as a plan and actually build it;

incompletely and imperfectly if necessary, rather than leave it as something only reasoned about in the abstract.

Whatever the Knuth comparison ends up showing, that part of the goal is already met.